## 7. Editing content in Sanity (and why you probably can't yet)

**Current reality: the Studio is live and connected, but nothing on the website reads from it.** Sam can log in, create documents, publish them, and see no change on the site. Setting expectations on this is the first conversation to have.

### For the client — using the Studio today

1. Go to `https://sanity.io/manage`, open the **RecSeekers Website** project (`3o1ax6lm`), or run the Studio locally with `cd cms && npm run dev` → `http://localhost:3333`.
2. Three document types are available: **Template Status** (a wiring smoke-test), **LinkedIn Embed** (a list of LinkedIn post URLs), and **LinkedIn Feed** (post content/URL/date/image).
3. Content only becomes visible to the frontend once **Published** — the site queries published documents through the CDN.

### For a developer — what wiring it up would take

The plumbing is already in place: `sanity/client.ts` (configured client), `sanity/queries.ts` (GROQ), `sanity/image.ts` (`urlFor` image builder), and `next.config.ts` already allows `cdn.sanity.io`. A page becomes CMS-driven with:

```tsx
import { client } from "@/sanity/client";

export default async function Page() {
  const data = await client.fetch(SOME_QUERY);   // add the query to sanity/queries.ts
  return <Section {...data} />;
}
```

The realistic scope of migrating this site to the CMS:

| Priority | Content | Difficulty |
|---|---|---|
| High | Team members (names, roles, bios, photos) | Easy — already a plain array in `TeamSection.tsx` |
| High | Testimonials | Easy — plain arrays, but consolidate the three forked components first |
| High | Legal pages | Easy — `legal.en.json` already has the right shape |
| Medium | "How It Works" steps, employer feature cards, `FitSplitCard` panels | Easy — plain arrays |
| Medium | Core values | Medium — the icons are **inline JSX inside the data array** and would need extracting to files or an icon-name enum |
| Low | Page hero copy | Medium — heavy mobile/desktop copy forking (see §8) means each block is really two |
| Hard | `WhereWeWorkSection` | Hard — descriptions are `ReactNode` with embedded `<Link>`s, and the maps are inline SVG paths |

**Recommendation:** decide this explicitly before doing anything else. The half-wired state is the main thing that will confuse the next developer. If the client won't be editing content, delete `cms/`, `frontend/sanity/`, and the three Sanity dependencies and the repo gets meaningfully simpler.

